repository-code: 'https://github.com/Mycology-Microbiology-Center/MultiSoil'
abstract: >-
  This study aimed to develop a multi-component soil health
  index for croplands and to identify agrochemical,
  functional and DNA-based indicators of soil health. In
  March-April, soil samples were collected from 30 winter
  wheat fields on mineral soils across Estonia, following
  the Soil BON sampling design. At each site, nine soil
  cores were taken from a 30 m x 30 m plot and pooled
  separately for the 0-10 cm and 0-20 cm depths. DNA was
  extracted from dried composite samples: two replicates
  from 0-10 cm and one from 0-20 cm, giving 90 samples in
  total. Prokaryotes were profiled by sequencing the 16S
  rRNA gene V4-V5 region (primers 515F/926R) on Illumina
  NovaSeq. Fungi and protists were profiled by sequencing
  the full-length ITS region (primers ITS9mun/ITS4ngsUni) on
  PacBio Revio. Soil animals were profiled by sequencing the
  mitochondrial COI gene (primers mlCOIintF/jgHCO2198) on
  Illumina NovaSeq. A subset of 0-10 cm DNA samples
  underwent shotgun metagenomic sequencing on Illumina
  NovaSeq. The metagenomes were used to quantify genes
  involved in nitrogen and sulphur cycling,
  carbohydrate-active enzymes and antibiotic resistance
  genes. The sequence data were analysed together with soil
  agrochemical, biogeochemical, microbial activity and
  nematode data to build the soil health index and evaluate
  candidate indicators. The study was funded by the EU
  Horizon project MultiSoil.                            
